# Grade 12 (part a): teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'12-1-1': dict(
  saint="Peter Faber: a shepherd boy from the Alps, the first priest of the Jesuits, who changed people by listening.",
  tryit=["Write what you are good at, what the world needs and what you love.", "Look for where they overlap.", "Name one next step to test it."],
  saintStory="Pierre Favre, or Peter Faber, was born in 1506 in a village in the Savoy Alps, to a poor family. As a boy, he kept the family’s sheep, and at about seven, he made a private vow of chastity. He was bright and was sent to study in Paris, where he shared a room at the Collège Sainte-Barbe with two others: the Basque soldier Ignatius of Loyola, and Francis Xavier, an ambitious young Navarrese.\nFaber was shy and anxious about his scruples. Ignatius helped him with patience and the Spiritual Exercises, and Faber became his first companion. He was ordained a priest in 1534 and was the one who celebrated the Mass at Montmartre where the little group took their vows. They were the first Jesuits.\nHe preached and gave retreats across Germany, Spain and Portugal, at a time of religious division. His method was to begin with friendship and listening, not argument. He died in Rome in 1546, at forty. Pope Francis, a Jesuit, called him a model and added him to the canon of saints in 2013.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If someone asked you right now, ‘What is God asking you to do with your life?’, what would you say? Is that a question you can answer? Who helps you?” Take honest answers, including ‘I don’t know.’",
    teach=["Say: “The word vocation comes from the Latin vocare, to call. The Church teaches that every person is called by God. The first call is universal: to know, love and serve God, and to be holy. Vatican II’s Lumen Gentium chapter 5 is titled ‘The Universal Call to Holiness.’ All Christians, ‘of whatever state or rank,’ are called to the fullness of Christian life and perfection of charity. This call comes in Baptism. Then there is a personal call: God has a particular plan for each of us. ‘Before I formed you in the womb I knew you, and before you were born I consecrated you.’ (Jeremiah 1:5) The saints say that the one is the path to the other: by being faithful where we are, we find our particular call.”", "Say: “How do we discern? Discernment is a skill of listening to God and acting wisely. Ignatius of Loyola taught that we notice interior movements: consolation (a deep, lasting peace and love of God that leads us to give ourselves) and desolation (restlessness, discouragement, being drawn away from God). We look at what is life-giving over time. We pray with Scripture. We examine our gifts, desires and the needs of the world. We seek counsel from wise people: parents, priests, mentors. We notice how God’s call often comes through ordinary things, desires and circumstances. And we make a choice and test it. God’s will is generally not a hidden secret to be uncovered like a code, but a path to be walked in freedom with prayer.”", "Say: “Some common difficulties. Fear: ‘What if I choose wrong?’ God is more concerned with our fidelity than a perfect answer. Many paths can be holy. Pressure: from parents, peers or culture. We need freedom. Doubt: ‘Am I good enough?’ Moses, Jeremiah and Mary all asked. Impatience: God often reveals the call one step at a time. The question is often not ‘Which is the one right life?’ but ‘What is the next faithful step?’ Peter Faber was anxious and scrupulous; he learned to trust. He changed people by friendship and attention. And Matthew 11:28: ‘Come to me, all who labor and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest.’ A vocation is not a burden but a gift.”"],
    ask=["What does the word ‘vocation’ mean? (A call.)", "What is the universal call? (To holiness, in Baptism.)", "What are consolation and desolation? (Ignatius’s names for movements toward and away from God.)"],
    practice=["Draw three overlapping circles: what I’m good at, what I love, what the world needs.", "Write three items in each.", "Look for overlaps and note them in the center.", "Choose one next step to test, such as a conversation, volunteering or a course, and write when you will do it."],
    close="Pray the Suscipe or: “Lord, show me your way and give me the courage to walk it.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Talk with someone you trust about your discernment.”")),

'12-1-2': dict(
  saint="The family of Basil the Great: married parents, a consecrated sister, a priest and two bishops, all honored as saints.",
  tryit=["Describe the four paths and what each gives to the Church.", "Name a saint for each.", "Write what attracts and what frightens you about each."],
  saintStory="In Cappadocia, in what is now Turkey, in the fourth century, a remarkable family produced a number of saints. Basil the Elder, a lawyer and teacher, and Emmelia were married and raised ten children. They had lost property in a persecution and kept their faith. Emmelia’s mother, Macrina the Elder, had lived through the persecution of Diocletian and taught her grandchildren the faith.\nTheir eldest daughter, Macrina, chose to be consecrated to God, and turned the family estate into a community for prayer and service. One son, Basil, became bishop of Caesarea, a great theologian and founder of monasteries. Another, Gregory, was married and later became bishop of Nyssa. Another, Peter, was a monk and then bishop of Sebaste. Naucratius, who died young, lived as a hermit and cared for the poor.\nSo from one household came a married couple, a consecrated woman, a hermit, a monk, and bishops. Together they show the variety of vocations in one family, all working toward one thing: holiness.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write on the board: Marriage, Priesthood, Consecrated life, Single life. Ask, “Which of these do you think is the ‘best’ vocation? Which is the ‘easiest’? Which is the one you know least about?” Take honest answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church has traditionally spoken of four states of life. Marriage: a man and a woman who give themselves to each other for life, open to children. It is a sacrament, a path of holiness through love, sacrifice, forgiveness and raising a family. Holy Orders: men called to serve as deacons, priests or bishops, to teach, sanctify and lead the Church. In the Latin Church, priests are celibate. Consecrated life: those who profess the evangelical counsels of poverty, chastity and obedience: monks, nuns, friars, sisters, consecrated virgins, hermits, members of secular institutes. They are a sign of the Kingdom and give their lives to prayer and service. Single life: those who remain unmarried, for various reasons, and live their baptismal vocation in service. Some make private commitments.”", "Say: “None is superior in dignity, though the Church teaches that consecrated virginity for the Kingdom is objectively a higher calling than marriage (Trent; Familiaris Consortio 16) in the sense that it anticipates the life of heaven, where ‘they neither marry nor are given in marriage.’ But for each person, the best vocation is the one God calls them to. Paul wrote: ‘Each has his own special gift from God, one of one kind and one of another.’ (1 Corinthians 7:7) Marriage and celibacy are not rivals. They need each other: families nurture vocations, and celibates remind married people of the Kingdom. Both are forms of love. The Church needs all.”", "Say: “What does each demand? Marriage asks fidelity, patience, forgiveness, openness to life, daily sacrifice. Priesthood asks availability, celibacy, obedience to the bishop, sacrifice of one’s own plans. Consecrated life asks radical detachment, community and obedience. Single life asks discipline to avoid isolation, and intentional community and service. All ask prayer and sacramental life. And all are, in different ways, a gift of self: love. The Church’s teaching is that the vocation must be chosen freely; no one should be pushed. Discerning a vocation typically takes time: a period of dating or a period of seminary or religious formation. If you are drawn to priesthood or religious life, contact a vocation director. If you’re drawn to marriage, prepare by growing in virtue and choosing well.”"],
    ask=["What are the four states of life? (Marriage, Holy Orders, consecrated life, single life.)", "Is any state a better path to holiness for everyone? (No; the best is the one God calls you to.)", "How does celibacy relate to marriage? (They complement each other.)"],
    practice=["Make a four-column chart: Marriage / Priesthood / Consecrated life / Single life.", "In each, write a saint, what it asks, what it gives the Church, and one question you have.", "Underline which attracts you and which frightens you.", "Share one honest reaction with a partner."],
    close="Pray for vocations of every kind: “Lord, give us holy families, priests, religious and single people who serve you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask a married person, a priest or sister, and a single adult how they knew.”")),

'12-1-3': dict(
  saint="Zita of Lucca: a domestic servant who sanctified every chore.",
  tryit=["Assess your gifts honestly.", "Match one gift to a need.", "Write how work can be prayer."],
  saintStory="Zita was born around 1212 in a village in Tuscany, to a poor, devout family. At twelve she was sent to work as a servant in the household of a wealthy cloth merchant, the Fatinelli family, in Lucca, and she stayed in that house for the rest of her life, about forty-eight years.\nShe was conscientious and did every task as if for God. She rose early to attend Mass before beginning work, and prayed as she worked. Her fellow servants resented her; the master and mistress were severe at first. Zita bore it patiently. She gave away food to the poor from the table, to the point of trouble, and once, according to a story, when found out with bread in her apron, it was turned to flowers. Another story is that while she prayed, angels baked the household’s bread.\nIn time, the family grew to respect and love her. She became the trusted head of the household. She died in 1272. She is patron of domestic workers.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What do you spend most of your week doing? What would it mean to say that this is a vocation?” Take answers. “Many people think of vocation as only priests and nuns. The Church says every kind of work can be holy.”",
    teach=["Say: “Genesis 2:15: ‘The Lord God took the man and put him in the garden of Eden to till it and keep it.’ Work is part of God’s plan, before the fall. After the fall, it became toilsome (Genesis 3:17-19), but it remains good. Jesus himself worked as a carpenter. Paul: ‘If anyone will not work, let him not eat.’ (2 Thessalonians 3:10) John Paul II’s Laborem Exercens (1981) teaches that work is ‘a fundamental dimension of human existence’ and that it is for the person, not the person for work. Through work we share in God’s creative activity, support ourselves and our families, serve the community and develop our gifts.”", "Say: “The Church also teaches about gifts. In 1 Corinthians 12, Paul describes charisms, gifts of the Holy Spirit for building up the Church: wisdom, knowledge, faith, healing, miracles, prophecy, discernment, tongues, interpretation; and in Romans 12, teaching, service, encouragement, giving, leading, mercy. ‘To each is given the manifestation of the Spirit for the common good.’ Everyone has gifts. Talents (Matthew 25:14-30) are given to be used and multiplied, not buried. A calling to a career involves our gifts, interests, values, opportunities and the needs of others. Parable of the talents shows that God expects us to risk and invest.”", "Say: “How can work be holy? Offer it to God in a morning offering. Do it well, with integrity, honesty and excellence (Colossians 3:23: ‘Whatever your task, work heartily, as serving the Lord and not men’). Treat colleagues with dignity. Avoid dishonest gain. Set limits: keep Sunday, avoid being a slave to work. Use income justly and generously. Josemaría Escrivá taught that ordinary work is a path to holiness. Zita shows that the meaning of work isn’t the status of the task but the love in which it is done. Choose a career asking: Will it let me serve? Will it respect my values and my future family? Vocation is not only what you do but how you live.”"],
    ask=["What does Laborem Exercens teach? (Work is for the person; it shares in God’s creative work.)", "What is a charism? (A gift of the Spirit for the common good.)", "How can work be holy? (Offer it to God, do it well, with integrity and love.)"],
    practice=["List five gifts you have (be honest and specific) and ask two others to name one more.", "Match one gift to a need in your school, parish or community.", "Write a morning offering for your work or studies.", "Choose one concrete way to use your gift this month."],
    close="Pray the Morning Offering. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Do one task well, as a prayer.”")),

'12-2-1': dict(
  saint="Blessed Nicolas Steno: a Danish scientist who founded modern geology and then became a bishop.",
  tryit=["Sort claims of conflict between faith and science.", "Mark which are real questions and which are myths.", "Write the Church’s principles."],
  saintStory="Niels Stensen, or Nicolas Steno, was born in Copenhagen in 1638 to a Lutheran family. He was a brilliant student of anatomy. At twenty-seven he discovered the duct of the parotid gland, still called Steno’s duct. He showed that the heart is a muscle. In Florence, studying a shark’s head, he noticed that its teeth resembled “tongue stones” found in rocks, and concluded that rocks had been formed by layers of sediment, and that fossils were remains of once-living creatures. He formulated principles of layering, which became the foundation of geology.\nAt the height of his scientific career, he experienced a spiritual crisis. He studied the faith and in 1667 was received into the Catholic Church in Florence. He gave up science, was ordained a priest in 1675, and was named a bishop in 1677 to serve the Church in northern Germany and Scandinavia. He lived in great poverty, giving away his possessions and his bishop’s ring.\nHe died in 1686, at forty-eight. He was beatified in 1988. He shows that a scientist can be a man of faith.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Is there a conflict between science and religion? Where do you get that idea? From a person, a movie, a class?” Take answers and write them. “Let’s test each claim.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that faith and reason cannot contradict, because the same God is the author of both. The First Vatican Council: ‘Though faith is above reason, there can never be any real discrepancy between faith and reason. Since the same God who reveals mysteries and infuses faith has bestowed the light of reason on the human mind, God cannot deny himself, nor can truth ever be in opposition to truth.’ (Dei Filius, as quoted in CCC 159) John Paul II: ‘Science can purify religion from error and superstition. Religion can purify science from idolatry and false absolutes.’ Both seek truth, using different methods and asking different questions.”", "Say: “Historical myths. The Galileo case (1633) was real, but is often exaggerated: it was a clash over the interpretation of Scripture and the state of evidence, not the Church’s opposition to science. The Church was a major patron of science: universities, observatories (the Vatican Observatory), priest-scientists like Copernicus (a canon), Mendel (a friar who founded genetics), Lemaître (the Big Bang), and Steno. ‘The Middle Ages believed in a flat earth’ is false; educated people knew the earth was round. The idea of a permanent war between faith and science comes largely from the 19th-century ‘conflict thesis’ and is rejected by historians of science. Real tensions exist: some people take Genesis as a science textbook (fundamentalism), and some use science to claim there is no God (scientism).”", "Say: “Hard questions today. Evolution: the Church allows that the human body may have evolved, but each soul is directly created by God (Pius XII, Humani Generis; John Paul II, 1996 message; Benedict XVI). The Church rejects materialistic evolution as a total worldview. Origins: the Big Bang and the age of the universe are compatible with creation, which is about the dependence of everything on God, not just the first moment. The mind: neuroscience studies brain activity, but the Church teaches that the spiritual soul is not reducible to matter. Miracles: not violations of nature, but events beyond nature’s power, signs. Creation is rational and ordered, and this is why science works. Many scientists believe in God: Francis Collins, John Lennox, George Coyne. Science investigates how; faith asks why. Be humble about both.”"],
    ask=["Can truth contradict truth? (No; the same God is author of both.)", "Name a scientist who was a Catholic priest or religious. (Steno, Mendel, Lemaître.)", "What does the Church say about evolution? (The body may have evolved; the soul is directly created.)"],
    practice=["List ten statements that people make about science and religion (e.g., ‘The Church burned scientists,’ ‘Evolution disproves God’).", "Sort them: myth, partly true, real question.", "Pick one real question and write how a Catholic scientist might answer.", "Write a one-paragraph principle: how faith and science relate."],
    close="Pray Psalm 19:1-4 or Psalm 8. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read the biography of a Catholic scientist.”")),

'12-2-2': dict(
  saint="G. K. Chesterton: a journalist who argued his way from doubt into the Church and kept laughing.",
  tryit=["State the strongest argument for atheism fairly.", "Give the strongest Catholic reply.", "Say what secularism misses."],
  saintStory="Gilbert Keith Chesterton was born in London in 1874. As a student, he flirted with skepticism and even the occult, and was depressed. He recovered and decided that existence was a gift, and that gratitude was the right response. He became a journalist, poet and novelist, a large man with a cape and a sword stick, known for his wit and paradoxes. He wrote around eighty books and thousands of essays.\nHis book Orthodoxy (1908) describes his journey to belief as like an explorer who sails to discover a new land and finds he has landed on his own country’s shore. He wrote the Father Brown mysteries featuring a priest-detective. He debated George Bernard Shaw and H. G. Wells with good humor. He was received into the Catholic Church in 1922.\nHe died in 1936. Pius XI named him Defender of the Faith. A cause for his canonization has been opened. He wrote, “The Christian ideal has not been tried and found wanting. It has been found difficult; and left untried.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What are the strongest reasons someone your age would give for not believing in God? Be honest. Write them all.” List them on the board. “We’ll take each seriously.”",
    teach=["Say: “Atheism is the denial of God’s existence. The Catechism (2123-2128) distinguishes: practical atheism (living as if God doesn’t exist), theoretical atheism (denying God, sometimes based on science, suffering, the problem of evil, or hypocrisy), agnosticism (we cannot know), and indifferentism. The Church says atheism ‘can have various origins’; believers can bear some responsibility ‘to the extent that they neglect their formation in the faith, or present its teaching in a misleading way, or even fail in their religious, moral, or social life.’ (CCC 2125) The Church doesn’t treat atheists as enemies; Vatican II’s Gaudium et Spes calls for dialogue with them.”", "Say: “Common arguments and replies. ‘Science has explained everything’: science describes how the world works; it does not answer why there is something rather than nothing, why the laws exist, or the meaning of life. ‘Evil proves there is no God’: this is the strongest argument; the Church replies that God permits evil because of freedom, brings good from it, and in Christ shares our suffering. ‘Religion causes wars’: some conflicts involve religion, but the worst atrocities of the 20th century were committed by atheist regimes, and much of religion’s impact is charity. ‘Religious people are hypocrites’: true, but this speaks of human sin, not God’s existence. ‘I can be good without God’: yes, people can be moral, but the question is the grounding of morality and meaning.’”", "Say: “Secularism is the view that public life should be separate from religion, or that religion is a private matter. Healthy secularity respects freedom of conscience and the autonomy of earthly things. But secularism as ideology wants to exclude faith from the public square or from any claim on truth. Features of modern secularism: individualism (me first), consumerism, relativism (‘there’s no truth’), and a loss of transcendence. Benedict XVI called this the ‘dictatorship of relativism.’ Many people are ‘nones’ (no religious affiliation), and the fastest-growing group is those raised in the faith who leave. The Church’s response: the New Evangelization. Give witness, ask good questions, offer beauty, truth and goodness, and answer honestly. Pope Benedict said many ‘seek God without knowing it.’ Chesterton’s laughter is part of it.”"],
    ask=["What are practical and theoretical atheism? (Living as if there is no God; claiming there is none.)", "How do believers contribute to unbelief? (By poor witness, formation or example.)", "What is relativism? (The view that there’s no objective truth.)"],
    practice=["Write the strongest argument for atheism in your own words, as fairly as you can.", "Write the best reply.", "List what secular culture offers and what it cannot provide.", "Role-play a respectful conversation between a believer and a skeptic friend."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, I believe; help my unbelief.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask a skeptical friend what they find most convincing and listen.”")),

'12-2-3': dict(
  saint="Blessed Alojzije Stepinac: an archbishop who sheltered the persecuted and was jailed for refusing to bow to the state.",
  tryit=["Distinguish religious freedom from relativism.", "State what the state may and may not do.", "Apply it to a case."],
  saintStory="Alojzije Stepinac was born in 1898 in Croatia, the eighth of eight children in a farming family. He served in the First World War, then studied for the priesthood in Rome. In 1937 he was named archbishop of Zagreb at thirty-nine. During the Second World War, Croatia was ruled by the fascist Ustaše regime, which persecuted Serbs, Jews and Roma. Stepinac spoke against it in sermons and letters. He sheltered Jews, helped hundreds escape, and condemned racial laws and the killing of civilians.\nWhen the communists took power in Yugoslavia, he refused to break with Rome and set up a national church, and protested the persecution of the Church. In 1946 he was arrested and put on trial in a show trial, accused of collaboration with the fascists. He was sentenced to sixteen years of hard labor. After five years in prison, he was put under house arrest in his home village, where he stayed until his death in 1960.\nPope Pius XII made him a cardinal in 1953. John Paul II beatified him in 1998. His case remains debated by historians.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Should a government be allowed to forbid a religion? To force one? What about a school requiring a prayer, or a hospital being forced to do something against its faith? Where is the line?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Vatican II’s Dignitatis Humanae (1965), the Declaration on Religious Freedom, taught that ‘the human person has a right to religious freedom. This freedom means that all men are to be immune from coercion on the part of individuals or of social groups and of any human power, in such wise that in matters religious no one is to be forced to act in a manner contrary to his own beliefs. Nor is anyone to be restrained from acting in accordance with his own beliefs, whether privately or publicly, whether alone or in association with others, within due limits.’ This is based on human dignity and the nature of faith, which must be free. This freedom is a civil right, not a claim that all religions are equally true.”", "Say: “This was a development in the Church’s expression. In earlier times, in Christendom, the state often enforced religious unity. The Church recognized more clearly that coercion in matters of faith is contrary to dignity (an idea found in Tertullian and Lactantius). John Courtney Murray, an American Jesuit, helped develop the teaching, drawing on the American experience. The Church still teaches that there’s one true religion and that all are bound to seek truth, but that nobody can be forced. Limits: public order and the common good. The state must protect religious freedom, but not necessarily endorse a religion. The Church requests freedom to worship, teach, run institutions, and act in public life according to conscience.”", "Say: “Pluralism: modern society includes many religions and worldviews. The Church affirms that Catholics can live in a pluralist society, cooperate with others on common goals, and respect other beliefs. We shouldn’t force our beliefs through coercion, but we should bring our convictions to public debate, with reasoned arguments accessible to all. Real contemporary issues: conscientious objection (healthcare workers, business owners), religious schools and charities, religious persecution abroad (Christians are the most persecuted religious group in the world), blasphemy laws, and public symbols. Religious freedom is under pressure in many countries. Stepinac’s life shows that totalitarian states often see the Church as a rival. The Church defends the freedom of all religions, not only her own.”"],
    ask=["What does Dignitatis Humanae teach? (Everyone has the right to religious freedom, free from coercion.)", "Does it teach all religions are equally true? (No; it’s about civil freedom.)", "What are limits on religious freedom? (Public order and the common good.)"],
    practice=["Prepare cases: a Catholic pharmacist and a prescription; a school board and a prayer; a country that bans conversion; a Catholic charity and a government requirement.", "For each, decide what religious freedom requires and what the common good might limit.", "Write a principle for a pluralist society.", "Share and debate with respect."],
    close="Pray for persecuted Christians and all who suffer for conscience. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look up a place where religious freedom is restricted.”")),

'12-3-1': dict(
  saint="Pierre Toussaint: a former slave from Haiti who became a successful hairdresser in New York and gave away his money.",
  tryit=["Apply subsidiarity to an example.", "Identify who should decide.", "Describe a way to participate."],
  saintStory="Pierre Toussaint was born in 1766, enslaved in Saint-Domingue (now Haiti). He was taught to read by his owner’s family, and was brought to New York in 1787 by his mistress, Madame Bérard. There he trained as a hairdresser and became popular with wealthy women. When her husband died, leaving her poor, Pierre supported her with his earnings, until she died in 1807 and freed him.\nAfter being freed, he continued to work hard and prospered. He and his wife Juliette, who was also freed, lived modestly. He gave generously: to orphans, to the sick during yellow fever epidemics, to refugees; he bought freedom for others; and he helped raise money for the first Catholic cathedral in New York, St. Patrick’s on Mott Street. He went to Mass every day.\nHe died in 1853. He is buried in the crypt at the new St. Patrick’s Cathedral. He was declared Venerable in 1996. His life shows the dignity of work and the power of a person who does good in his own sphere.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Who should decide how a school lunch program runs: the federal government, the state, the school board, the principal, or the parents? Why?” Take answers. “Catholic social teaching has a principle about this.”",
    teach=["Say: “Subsidiarity is a key principle of Catholic social teaching. Pius XI in Quadragesimo Anno (1931): ‘It is an injustice and at the same time a grave evil and disturbance of right order to assign to a greater and higher association what lesser and subordinate organizations can do.’ The Catechism: ‘A community of a higher order should not interfere in the internal life of a community of a lower order, depriving the latter of its functions, but rather should support it in case of need.’ (CCC 1883) The family, local communities and voluntary associations (the ‘mediating structures’) should be respected. The state should help, not replace. At the same time, higher levels should step in when lower ones cannot.”", "Say: “Subsidiarity has two sides: it limits intervention, and it requires help. If a family is struggling, the neighborhood, parish and charity should help; if they can’t, the city, state or nation should. It protects freedom and responsibility, and recognizes that decisions are best made by those closest to the situation. It opposes both excessive centralization (totalitarianism, bureaucratic over-control) and a negligent neglect of the vulnerable. In the Church, subsidiarity applies too: the diocese and parish have their roles, and the Pope does not micromanage. Also in economics: small businesses, cooperatives and local solutions.”", "Say: “Participation: ‘It is a requirement of human dignity that people take an active part in public life.’ (GS 75) Everyone has a right and a duty to contribute to the common good: voting, serving on boards, volunteering, paying taxes, running for office, speaking up. Ways Pierre Toussaint participated: through his work, giving, and building institutions. Obstacles: poverty, discrimination, apathy, cynicism. The Church encourages Catholics to engage in politics from their conscience. She doesn’t tell people how to vote, but forms conscience on principles: the dignity of life, family, the poor, the common good. In the U.S., Faithful Citizenship sets this out. For young people: register to vote, learn the issues, volunteer locally, join a community group. Citizenship is a form of charity.”"],
    ask=["What is subsidiarity? (Decisions should be made at the lowest competent level, with higher levels helping when needed.)", "What is participation? (Everyone’s right and duty to take part in public life.)", "Whom did Pierre Toussaint help? (Orphans, the sick, refugees, enslaved people, the Church.)"],
    practice=["Choose three problems: a school bullying issue, a food shortage in the town, a national healthcare policy.", "For each, decide who should act first and who should help.", "Write one way you can participate in public life this year.", "Share with a partner."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, help me to do my part for the common good.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Find out how to register to vote or join a community group.”")),

'12-3-2': dict(
  saint="Blessed Giuseppe Toniolo: an economics professor and father of seven who taught that economics must serve people.",
  tryit=["Compare three economic systems by Church principles.", "Mark what each gets right and wrong.", "Write a just-wage principle."],
  saintStory="Giuseppe Toniolo was born in 1845 in Treviso, Italy, and became a professor of economics at the University of Pisa. He was a devout Catholic, a layman, and the father of seven children. At a time when many Catholic intellectuals felt cut off from the modern world, he insisted that Catholics should study economics and social sciences seriously, and that economic life should be guided by ethics.\nHe founded the Catholic Union for Social Studies in 1889, which organized the Catholic Social Weeks in Italy, and he influenced Leo XIII’s Rerum Novarum. He promoted cooperatives, rural banks, workers’ unions, and fair wages. He wrote that the economy should serve human beings, and he argued against both unregulated liberalism and socialism.\nHe lived a simple life, going to Mass every day, and loved his family. He died in 1918 and was beatified in 2012. He shows that economics can be a path to holiness.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What makes an economy good? Is it growth, jobs, equality, or something else? Who is it for?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Gaudium et Spes 63: ‘Man is the source, the focus and the end of all economic and social life.’ The Church teaches that the economy exists for people, not people for the economy. The Compendium of the Social Doctrine states principles for economic life. Work has priority over capital: work is an expression of the person. Just wage: ‘The just wage is the legitimate fruit of work.’ (CCC 2434) A wage should be enough to support a worker and family in dignity. Rights of workers: safe conditions, rest, the right to organize in unions, fair treatment. Property: private property is legitimate, but with a social purpose. Business: should seek profit, but also serve the community and respect workers. Consumers: have duties too, including avoiding waste and unfair purchases.”", "Say: “The Church has criticized various systems. Marxism and communism: denies God, abolishes private property, class struggle; condemned by Pius IX, Leo XIII, Pius XI. Unrestrained capitalism or liberalism: individualism, profit as the only goal, commodifying persons; criticized in Rerum Novarum and Quadragesimo Anno, and by Pope Francis (‘an economy of exclusion’; ‘idolatry of money’). The Church says: no system is perfect. Markets are good for efficiency, but they need moral and legal framework: ‘The market is not automatically just.’ Centesimus Annus (1991) by John Paul II after the fall of communism: affirmed the free market and private enterprise, in a context of strong ethics and law, and criticized consumerism. Caritas in Veritate (2009) by Benedict XVI: the economy needs gratuity and fraternity, not only contracts.”", "Say: “Particular questions. Poverty and inequality: the Church teaches preferential concern for the poor. Debt of poor nations and fair trade. Taxes and the role of government: government has a role in justice, but subsidiarity limits. Globalization: benefits and risks. Usury, gambling, advertising and consumerism. Environment. Employers and employees: the duty to treat people as persons. Immigration: the right to emigrate for a decent life; nations have the right to regulate borders for the common good, but must welcome as far as possible. Individual Catholics can disagree on policy while sharing principles. Your own habits: how you spend, save and give. Tithing and almsgiving. Think about what you buy, who made it, and what it costs. Toniolo’s lesson: economics is a moral science; study it.”"],
    ask=["What is the economy for? (The person and the common good.)", "What is a just wage? (Enough to support a worker and family in dignity.)", "What did Centesimus Annus say of the free market? (Good within a moral and legal framework.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart comparing communism, unregulated capitalism and the Church’s approach, using three principles: dignity, property, work.", "Find one product you use and trace who made it and in what conditions.", "Write a personal ‘economic ethic’ of five commitments.", "Share one."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, give us a just economy that serves all.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Tithe or give a portion of what you earn to the poor.”")),

'12-3-3': dict(
  saint="Maximilian of Tebessa: a twenty-one-year-old who refused to be a soldier and was beheaded.",
  tryit=["Test a case against the just war criteria.", "Mark which fail.", "Write what peacemaking asks of you."],
  saintStory="Maximilian was a young man in Numidia, in North Africa, in AD 295. He was the son of a veteran soldier named Fabius Victor, and as such was required to enlist in the Roman army. His father brought him before the proconsul Dion in Tebessa (Theveste) for the military enrollment. The record of the trial, called the Acts of Maximilian, is one of the oldest documents about Christians and military service.\nWhen the recruiting officer was about to put a lead seal around his neck, the sign of enlistment, Maximilian said, “I cannot serve as a soldier. I cannot do evil. I am a Christian.” The proconsul said, “Serve and receive the seal.” Maximilian said, “I will not receive the seal. I already have the seal of Christ.”\nThey tried to persuade him, and his father urged him to comply. He refused. The proconsul sentenced him to death. Maximilian said, “Thanks be to God.” He was beheaded on March 12, 295. His father returned home, giving thanks that he had given such a gift to God. He is considered the patron of conscientious objectors.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Is it ever right to use force? To go to war? To refuse to fight? What is the best argument on each side?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church’s teaching on war and peace: the Catechism 2302-2317. Peace is not just the absence of war, but ‘the tranquility of order’ (Augustine), the work of justice and charity. ‘Blessed are the peacemakers.’ (Matthew 5:9) Anger and hatred are against the fifth commandment. War is always an evil, but the Church holds that governments have the right and duty of legitimate defense once peaceful efforts have failed. Pacifism is a legitimate personal vocation for those who choose non-violence, as Maximilian did, and the Church honors conscientious objectors. But the Church does not teach that all use of force is wrong.”", "Say: “Just war theory, from Augustine and Aquinas. The conditions for legitimate defense by military force are: the damage inflicted by the aggressor must be lasting, grave and certain; all other means must have been shown to be impractical or ineffective; there must be serious prospects of success; the use of arms must not produce evils graver than the evil to be eliminated. (CCC 2309) Also: just cause, legitimate authority, right intention. In conduct (jus in bello): noncombatants must be protected; the means must be proportionate; some acts are never allowed: indiscriminate destruction of cities, torture, targeting civilians. Vatican II condemned total war: ‘Any act of war aimed indiscriminately at the destruction of entire cities or extensive areas along with their populations is a crime against God and man.’ (GS 80)”", "Say: “Modern questions. Nuclear weapons: the Church condemns their use, and has been skeptical of deterrence as a long-term solution; Pope Francis has called possession of nuclear weapons immoral. Terrorism: always wrong. The arms race: ‘an utterly treacherous trap for humanity.’ (GS 81) Humanitarian intervention: ‘responsibility to protect.’ The Pope’s role: John Paul II opposed the 2003 Iraq war. Military service: Catholics may serve honorably; soldiers must refuse unlawful orders. Peace-building: dialogue, justice, development, forgiveness. ‘Peace is the work of justice’ (Isaiah 32:17). Paul VI: ‘If you want peace, work for justice.’ Peace begins in our hearts and homes: how we handle conflicts, forgive and speak. Pope Francis: ‘War is always a defeat for humanity.’ Pray, fast and act for peace.”"],
    ask=["Does the Church teach that war is always wrong? (No; she teaches legitimate defense under strict conditions.)", "Name three conditions of just war. (Just cause, last resort, probability of success, proportionality.)", "What does the Church say of conscientious objectors? (Honors them.)"],
    practice=["Choose a historical or current conflict.", "Test it against the just war criteria in a table: which are met, which not.", "Write a paragraph on what the Church would ask for in this case.", "Write one concrete act of peacemaking you can do in your own life."],
    close="Pray the Prayer of Saint Francis or the Litany for Peace. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Make peace with someone with whom you have a conflict.”")),
}
